 ## Overview
-Line Integral Convolution (LIC) is a texture-based flow visualization method that maps a scalar noise field onto a vector field by integrating the noise along streamlines, producing a streak-like texture that reveals global flow direction [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. It differs from streamline-based methods by densely displaying direction information across the entire field without requiring user-defined seed points, though it does not inherently encode flow direction (positive/negative) or speed magnitude [S3].
+Line Integral Convolution (LIC) is a texture-based flow visualization method that maps a scalar noise field onto a vector field by integrating the noise along streamlines, producing a streak-like texture that reveals global flow direction [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It differs from streamline-based methods by densely displaying direction information across the entire field without requiring user-defined seed points, though it does not inherently encode flow direction (positive/negative) or speed magnitude [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input 1:** A vector field $\mathbf{V}(x,y)$ defined on a 2D grid [S1, S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Input 2:** A scalar noise texture $F_{in}$ (typically white noise) with the same resolution as the vector field [S1, S2, unofficial][S3].
-*   **Output:** A scalar field $F_{out}$ representing the convolved texture [S1, S2, unofficial][S3].
+*   **Input 1:** A vector field $\mathbf{V}(x,y)$ defined on a 2D grid [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input 2:** A scalar noise texture $F_{in}$ (typically white noise) with the same resolution as the vector field [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A scalar field $F_{out}$ representing the convolved texture [S1, unofficial].
 
